NOT-A-SECRET. This file is bait for the ETHR web-server canary.

It is the one that predicts the deployment. The shared-hosting .htaccess blocks
api/.env, .git/, composer.json and storage/ with `RewriteRule ... [F,L]` — a
mod_rewrite [F] — and this file is blocked by that same mechanism. Its sibling
secret.txt.probe is blocked by <FilesMatch>/Require all denied instead, which
the deployment does not use. A host can honour one and not the other, so read
both.

If you can read THIS over HTTP, the deployment's own deny rules are not being
enforced, and api/.env would be web-readable in production while the
application still appeared to work.

There is no remedy available on this account. Plesk's "Additional nginx
directives" field does not exist on this plan (G0-A, read twice), and
deployment/SHARED-HOSTING-CONTRACT.md forbids requiring it in any case. The
layout already keeps ~/ethr outside the document root, which is what makes this
defence-in-depth rather than the only barrier — but a 200 here means the depth
is one layer thinner than the runbook assumes. Record it in
docs/deployment/GATE-0-RESULT.md as G0-B.3 FAIL and read that file's coverage
table before deploying.

Nothing here is confidential.
